# UI component rules

These are traps that cost real time in this crate. They are all about chrome — the strips,
tabs and toolbars around content — because that is where inconsistency is visible and where
call sites keep re-deriving values that already exist.

* Never hardcode a pane-level corner radius. `rounded_lg`, `px(8.)` in a `paint_quad` and
  `rounded_t_lg` used to all mean "the pane radius" in different files and drifted apart. Use
  `pane_corner_radius()` (or `pane_corner_radius_px(window)` at paint time) for the pane, its
  tab strip, and any content clipped against those corners.

* Island strips (a pane's document tabs, the right dock's panel switcher, a panel's own
  sub-tabs) use `TabStyle::Pill` on both the `TabBar` and every `Tab` in it. Continuity with
  the content was too subtle to tell which tab was active; the pill marks it with fill, accent
  border and accent icon instead. A pill strip hugs its tabs, so whatever must span the rest of
  the bar (a pane's "after the last tab" drop target) goes in `TabBar::after_tabs`, not in the
  strip. `TabStyle::Surface` is only for a tab that has to continue its content.

* A selected `Surface` `Tab` must be told the surface it covers, via `Tab::surface`. Without it the tab
  falls back to `tab_active_background`, which is continuous with the content only in themes
  that set the two equal — Zed's bundled themes do, many third-party ones (Tokyo Night Storm,
  for one) do not, and the tab then reads as a chip floating above unrelated content. A pane
  passes its item's `Item::content_background`; a panel's own sub-tabs pass the panel
  background.

* An item that paints its own background must implement `Item::content_background`, or its
  pane's active tab will be painted with the wrong colour. `TerminalView` is the example:
  themes are free to make `terminal_background` differ from `editor_background`.

* Do not spell out spacing in chrome with the Tailwind helpers (`.p_2()`, `.gap_1()`). They
  are fixed rems and do not respond to UI density, so a header built with them drifts out of
  alignment with the `DynamicSpacing`-based `HeaderBar`/`TabBar`/`Tab` next to it at any
  density other than Default. Use `HeaderBar::slot_padding`/`slot_gap`/`height`, or
  `DynamicSpacing` directly.

* Any strip at the top or bottom of a pane is a `HeaderBar` (`HeaderBar::footer` for the
  bottom one), not a hand-rolled `h_flex` with a literal height. Neighbouring panes are
  expected to line their strips up horizontally, which only holds if they share the metrics.
  A bar whose content wraps must call `wrapping(true)`, or the wrapped rows are clipped.

* An `IconButton` placed in a `Tab` slot needs `size(ButtonSize::None)` and
  `shape(IconButtonShape::Square)`. The default button size adds padding that overflows the
  14px slot and visually collides with the neighbouring tab.

* Icon size follows the role, not the taste of the call site: `IconSize::Small` for chrome
  inside a header or a tab, `IconSize::Medium` only for a primary action row such as a
  device/tool strip, `IconSize::XSmall` for indicators. A single pane showing three sizes is
  the symptom that this was decided per call site.
